          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which drug classes does heart failure guidance describe for reduced ejection fraction?",
          "answer": "The heart failure guidance says there are **four foundational drug classes** for reduced ejection fraction (40 percent or below). Current guidance describes them as started together or in rapid sequence rather than added one at a time over months. The passage I retrieved stops before listing them, so I can't name the four from these results.\n\nTwo related points are in the retrieved text:\n\n- **SGLT-2 inhibitors** are mentioned in the heart failure guidance (*Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: the four foundational therapy classes*). That document says they are the class with proven benefit in preserved ejection fraction, and it warns against applying the reduced-group therapy to preserved ejection fraction without care.\n- The diabetes guidance (*Type 2 diabetes: first-line pharmacologic management*) lists heart failure, particularly with reduced ejection fraction, as a condition where agents with proven organ benefit are preferred, and it names SGLT-2 inhibitors among them.\n\nThe retrieved results do not include the full list of the four classes. To get that, a follow-up search of the heart failure guidance for its class list would be needed, or a clinician can go through the options. Questions about which treatment fits a particular heart condition are best discussed with the treating clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
